<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    WP-2.1 - the Android SDL3 spike.

    DELIBERATELY NOT IN Karawan.sln. The plan calls this "timeboxed" and says the
    deliverable is an answer, not a feature. Keeping it out of the solution means it
    can never break AC-GLOBAL-1 for an unrelated work package, and it can be deleted
    in one `rm -rf` once GATE-A has given its answer.

    It is also NOT a MAUI app, unlike Wuka. MAUI is irrelevant to the question being
    asked (does SDL3 give us a GL context, input and IME on a real device?) and its
    shell/activity indirection is exactly the sort of thing that turns "the spike
    failed" into "something in the spike failed". Plain .NET Android, one activity.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-android</TargetFramework>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion>26.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>

    <ApplicationId>de.nassau_records.sdl3spike</ApplicationId>
    <ApplicationVersion>1</ApplicationVersion>
    <ApplicationDisplayVersion>1.0</ApplicationDisplayVersion>

    <!--
      64-bit is what Google Play's 16 KB rule applies to; armeabi-v7a is carried
      because Karawan.Natives ships it and leaving it out would make the APK's ABI set
      differ from Wuka's, which is the thing this spike stands in for.

      RuntimeIdentifiers, NOT AndroidSupportedAbis. The latter is what a search will
      suggest and it is silently ineffective on .NET 9 - it only emits
      "warning XA0036: the AndroidSupportedAbis MSBuild property is no longer
      supported", then builds the DEFAULT abi set (arm64-v8a + x86_64). The first
      version of this file used it, and the resulting APK shipped x86_64 instead of the
      armeabi-v7a it asked for.
    -->
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>android-arm64;android-arm</RuntimeIdentifiers>

    <!--
      The spike must fail loudly on exactly the two defects Phase 1 was about, so a
      green build here means something. WP-1.6 promoted XA4301 in Wuka for the same
      reason; XA0141 could NOT be promoted there because Silk's libSDL2.so is 4 KB.
      Here there is no Silk at all, so XA0141 is promoted too - and that is the
      single most informative assertion in this project.
    -->
    <MSBuildWarningsAsErrors>$(MSBuildWarningsAsErrors);XA4301;XA0141</MSBuildWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The C# bindings. Source-only; the native comes from Karawan.Natives. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Platform.SDL3\Platform.SDL3.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      libSDL3.so (+ libc++_shared.so) for both ABIs, from our own pinned CI build.
      Karawan.Natives' .aar uses the jni/<abi>/ layout .NET Android expects, so no
      per-file wiring is needed here - referencing the package is enough.

      ExcludeAssets="native" IS REQUIRED, and this build proved it rather than assumed
      it. Without it, RID fallback packages the package's runtimes/linux-x64/native/
      libSDL3.so and libopenal.so into the APK - glibc-linked x64 binaries that cannot
      load on Android, and neither of them 16 KB aligned. Promoting XA0141 in this
      project turned that into two build errors instead of two warnings nobody reads:

        error XA0141: ... 'libSDL3.so' ... contains 'runtimes/linux-x64/native/libSDL3.so'
        error XA0141: ... 'libopenal.so' ... contains 'runtimes/linux-x64/native/libopenal.so'

      This is the SAME defect class WP-0.3 found in ImGui.NET (a linux-x64 libcimgui.so
      landing in lib/x86_64/) and exactly what plan AC-1.3 forbids. "native" excludes
      runtimes/<rid>/native/ but NOT lib/<tfm>/*.aar, so the Android payload still
      arrives - which is why this one attribute is enough.

      FOLLOW-UP for WP-1.4/2.2: better to fix this in the package than in every consumer.
      Recorded in the WP-2.1 PR.

      ExcludeAssets="all" + an explicit AndroidLibrary below, rather than just letting
      the PackageReference deliver the .aar, because KARAWAN.NATIVES 0.1.0's ANDROID
      PAYLOAD IS NOT CONSUMABLE AS PACKAGED. NuGet resolves this package to
      lib/netstandard2.0/_._ for a net9.0-android project and never selects
      lib/net9.0-android34.0/Karawan.Natives.Android.aar:

        warning NU1701: Package 'Karawan.Natives 0.1.0' was restored using
        '.NETFramework,Version=v4.6.1, ...' instead of the project target framework
        'net9.0-android35.0'.

        project.assets.json:  compile -> lib/netstandard2.0/_._
                              runtime -> lib/netstandard2.0/_._

      The cause is visible by diffing against a package whose .aar delivery works:
      Silk.NET.Windowing.Sdl ships lib/net7.0-android33.0/{...aar, ...dll} - an
      ASSEMBLY ALONGSIDE THE AAR. Ours ships the .aar alone, so NuGet does not count
      net9.0-android34.0 among the package's supported frameworks and falls back.

      This build is what caught it. WP-1.5 could not have: it excludes the package's
      Android assets outright for unrelated reasons, so the defect sat behind an
      exclusion nobody had cause to lift.

      Fixing it properly means republishing the package (an irreversible, human-gated
      step under plan 2.5), so the spike points straight at the .aar file instead. That
      is a WORKAROUND, not the fix - see the WP-2.1 PR for the WP-1.4 follow-up.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Karawan.Natives" ExcludeAssets="all" GeneratePathProperty="true" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      SDL's Java glue, vendored from the SAME SDL commit as libSDL3.so (see
      java/PROVENANCE.md). It is NOT in Karawan.Natives yet - that package ships
      only .so files - and shipping the two halves from one pin is the right
      long-term fix, recorded in the WP-2.1 PR as follow-up for WP-2.2.

      Bind="true" generates the C# binding, which is what lets SdlSpikeActivity
      derive from org.libsdl.app.SDLActivity.
    -->
    <AndroidJavaSource Include="java\**\*.java" Bind="true" />

    <!-- Trims four unbindable fields out of the generated binding; see the file. -->
    <TransformFile Include="Transforms\Metadata.xml" />

    <!--
      The Android half of Karawan.Natives, referenced as a FILE because the package
      cannot deliver it (see the PackageReference above). $(PkgKarawan_Natives) comes
      from GeneratePathProperty="true". .NET Android extracts jni/<abi>/*.so from an
      AndroidLibrary .aar, which is how libSDL3.so and libc++_shared.so reach the APK.
    -->
    <AndroidLibrary Include="$(PkgKarawan_Natives)\lib\net9.0-android34.0\Karawan.Natives.Android.aar" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      libmain.so: the SDL_main <-> managed bridge. SDL's Java glue calls
      nativeRunMain("libmain.so", "SDL_main", ...), and libSDL3.so does not export
      SDL_main. Built by recipes/build-mainshim.sh; see that script for the full
      explanation and for why these binaries are checked in for the spike rather
      than pulled from a package.

      THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO <AndroidNativeLibrary Include=...> HERE. The .NET Android
      SDK already globs **/*.so into AndroidNativeLibrary by default and infers the ABI
      from the parent directory name, so natives/arm64-v8a/libmain.so is picked up on
      its own. Adding an explicit item as well includes each library TWICE, which the
      XA4301 promoted above turns into a build error:

        error XA4301: APK already contains the item lib/arm64-v8a/libmain.so; ignoring.

      Verified with `dotnet msbuild -getItem:AndroidNativeLibrary`, which showed each
      path listed twice. Worth knowing before WP-2.2 touches Wuka.csproj, which DOES
      list its natives explicitly - under Platforms/Android/android/<abi>/, a path the
      default glob also matches.
    -->
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
